  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in V.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(t),q(t)\\in V,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(t)+q(t)=q(t)+p(t).\n$$\n\nHence, the addition of polynomials in $V$ is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
